      "repos_url": "https://api.github.com/users/austinh/repos",
      "events_url": "https://api.github.com/users/austinh/events{/privacy}",
      "received_events_url": "https://api.github.com/users/austinh/received_events",
      "type": "User",
      "user_view_type": "public",
      "site_admin": false
    },
    "created_at": "2026-09-16T21:45:49Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-09-16T21:45:49Z",
    "body": "> I agree it makes sense to not load anything when an auto-size image stops being rendered.\n> \n> Not loading anything initially if it's not rendered could cause compat issues at this point, and is not what `sizes` is meant to do. How about assuming a layout size of the `width` and `height` attributes (on the `img` or the selected `source`), if present, otherwise [300x150](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#img-contain-size)?\n> \n> If one wants to avoid an initial wrong load until the element is rendered, `loading=lazy` can be used, or we can revisit [adding `loading=after-layout`](https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/8007).\n\nThis issue still happens with loading=lazy. In fact, auto sizes can only ever be loading= lazy. The issue here is that Chrome seems to reset the sizing and not listen to the fallbacks listed in sizes attribute. Webkit and firefox seem to be fine and their implementations don't load another image. \n\nSee here https://issues.chromium.org/issues/535260626#comment8",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
